  "era": "Becky Sharp to the dye-transfer shutdown",
  "summary": "Saturated three-strip color where every red is a costume decision.",
  "description": "Three-strip Technicolor (1932-1955) is cinema's most committed color system: a beam-splitting camera shot three film records through filters, dyes were imbibition-printed in a dedicated lab, and studio color consultants (the Technicolor \"color supervisor\" role — Natalie Kalmus above all) forced art direction, costumes and even actor makeup to serve the dye gamut. Results: the poppy fields of The Wizard of Oz, the ink-lagoon of The Adventures of Robin Hood, MGM's chorus-line reds. Modern digital \"technicolor\" grades quote its healthy skin tones and deep primaries; the original dye-transfer prints remain collector icons of depth and stability.",
  "culturalContext": "Technicolor was Hollywood's color doctrine: the lab's supervisors controlled every frame's palette, the dye-transfer prints became collector icons of depth, and \"glorious Technicolor\" remains the metaphor for cinema color at its most committed — quoted by every digital \"technicolor\" LUT.",
